    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d19">

                <pb facs="0020" n="16" xml:id="pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/1307: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-04-30T14:00:00-04:00">April 30, 1938—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. Your 225, April 30, noon. The Department approves your instructing various
                    offices as proposed. The Department suggests that, unless you perceive
                    objection, you instruct Caldwell<anchor n="24" xml:id="d19-f1"/> to assume
                    primary charge, under your direction, of coordinating the American study of this
                    subject and to keep in touch with Sansom<anchor n="25" xml:id="d19-f2"/> and
                    Hall-Patch while they are in north China. If you concur in the procedure
                    outlined above, please issue appropriate instructions and repeat the foregoing
                    to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d19-f1" n="24" xml:id="d19-f1-content">
                <p>John K. Caldwell, Consul General at Tientsin.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d19-f2" n="25" xml:id="d19-f2-content">
                <p>Sir George B. Sansom, Commercial Counselor of the British Embassy in Japan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
